{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgrRYvxNjvuYw83J9CJkR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the bug in `src/lru.py`, but I couldn't run it to confirm. My first test run failed on an import-path mistake. The retry was denied because Bash isn't permitted in this mode.\n\n**Cause:** `get()` returned the value without marking the key as recently used. After `put(1)`, `put(2)`, `get(1)`, key 1 was still the oldest entry, so `put(3)` evicted it.\n\n**Fix:** `get()` now moves the key to the most-recent end of `self.order` before returning the value:\n\n```python\ndef get(self, key):\n    if key not in self.data:\n        return None\n    self.order.remove(key)\n    self.order.append(key)\n    return self.data[key]\n```\n\nTracing your scenario by hand, `get(1)` leaves the order as `[2, 1]`. `put(3)` then evicts key 2, and `get(1)` returns `'a'`.\n\n`order` is a list, so `get` and `put` on an existing key are O(n). That's fine for small caches. For large ones, `collections.OrderedDict` with `move_to_end` would make them O(1). I can switch to that if you want.\n\nTo check it yourself, run this from the repo root:\n\n`PYTHONPATH=src python3 -c \"from lru import LRUCache; c=LRUCache(2); c.put(1,'a'); c.put(2,'b'); c.get(1); c.put(3,'c'); print(c.get(1), c.get(2), c.get(3))\"`\n\nIt should print `a None c`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":500,"cache_read_input_tokens":31684,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":500},"output_tokens":493,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b94d5ee6-1a89-4bd5-9215-769662a11809","uuid":"33c0025b-9fb3-4f25-9dfa-22005ee4cd8b","timestamp":"2026-11-09T00:41:00.216Z","request_id":"req_011C